<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kengtung State</h1><p class="st-meta">1243 CE – 1959 CE (approx.) · capital Kengtung</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The largest of the Shan states, in Myanmar's far east near China, Laos and Thailand, with close cultural ties to Lan Na.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tai-khun-people">Tai Khun</a> Chiang Tung, was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khun-people">Tai Khun</a> people and, according to tradition, brought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai of Lan Na</a> in the 13th century, who installed a governor there. Its ruling house dates from this period, and the city stands by Naung Tung lake.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung's</a> Khun culture, script and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> were closely related to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sipsong-panna">Chiang Hung</a>, and its rulers balanced between Lan Na, Burmese kings and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. From the 16th century it was a tributary of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> and later Konbaung, while trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, horses and opium across the borderlands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> came under British protection in 1890 and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a>. The saopha's powers ended in 1959, and the Burmese military demolished his palace in 1991. Kengtung remains the main town of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kengtung-state.jpg" alt="Kengtung State" width="320" height="368"><figcaption>Image: User:Jolle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan_States-Map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1243 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kengtung</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sao Kawng Kiao Intaleng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tai Khun</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kengtung, Chiang Tung, Keng Tung</dd></dl></aside></div>

